y can  do just that  and vice versa if it's a close game and  there's a lot of pawns in the middle and  we'll go over that in just a second if  the bishops can't move around they're  kind of worthless so a lot of times  you'll hear people say you not prefer  bishops over Knights or I prefer knights  and bishops well it really depends in  the situation of the game because if  it's a close game a bishop pair is not  really that great of an advantage but  early on if you have a bishop pair a lot  of people say that's an advantage  because a lot of people can easily open  up the game it's later on when the game  is closed that people will then prefer  Knights / bishops because bishops can't  move around any more so in this case  this is an open game as we saw there's  still a lot of room for the pawns to  move and the center is not locked up so  bishops are going to reign supreme and  bishops are going to be more valuable  than Knights even though they're both  three points so go ahead and take a look  it's blacks move here and what would you  say would be black's best move here  thinking along the lines of what we just  talked about bishops being more  important than  nights go and take them if you want to  pause it and the best move hopefully you  came up with is night to be four and  this is attacking this Bishop on d3  which is a very good Bishop the  LightSquared Bishop is one of the more  important minor pieces for white and at  the same time this Bishop is very good  because it's an open game and vice versa  this Knight is not as good that's not  saying it's bad but it's not as good as  a bishop because it is an open game as  you can see there's nowhere for the  bishop to move without this trade  happening you can't move to c4 he can be  captured and they can't move along this  diagonal here or else he's going to be  captured as well in this situation  you're going to be trading a knight for  a bishop so you're trading a three point  piece for a three point piece